WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.089
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.089 --> 00:00:04.400
I wanna talk today about the refugee crisis
going on around the world but let's begin

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.510
with an old saying often attributed to that
great expert in the field of human evil, Joseph

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:10.510
Stalin.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.030
"When one man dies, that's a tragedy.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.360
When thousands die, that's a statistic."

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.420
Large numbers can feel cold and distant and
even kind of comforting because they don't

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.420
feel like people.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:22.730
And I think that's one of the reasons much
of the world was able to ignore the years

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.449
old Syrian refugee crisis until recently.

00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:29.300
But then, after thousands of refugees died
this year trying to get to Europe, one three

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.300
year old boy's body washed ashore in Turkey.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.590
His name was Aylan Kurdi, and he drowned with
his five year old brother and his mom trying

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:37.590
to get to Greece.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:42.010
His father, Abdullah, survived and now has
returned to Syria to bury his wife and children.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:46.380
In fact, when offered the opportunity to resettle
in another country Abdullah said, "Now I don't

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:47.410
want anything.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.200
What was precious is gone."

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.469
To talk about the refugee crisis, we need
statistics, but let us not forget what is

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:54.469
precious.

00:00:54.469 --> 00:00:57.989
So for the past four and a half years, there
has been a horrific civil war in Syria, which

00:00:57.989 --> 00:01:01.820
began with the hope of the 2011 spring protests.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.989
Several dictatorships were toppled during
the Arab spring, although, some have since

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:06.390
ended up with new dictators.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:10.900
In Syria, long reigning dictator Bashar al-Assad
has refused to relinquish power and instead

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:16.091
has battled the rebellion with astonishing
violence, including torturing children, and

00:01:16.091 --> 00:01:18.619
gassing his own people with chemical weapons.

00:01:18.619 --> 00:01:22.799
So, back in 2011, Syria had a population of
22.4 million people.

00:01:22.799 --> 00:01:24.130
Here's what it looks like today.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:29.319
More than 250,000 people have been killed,
about 10.6 million Syrians, less than half

00:01:29.319 --> 00:01:33.520
the population, still live in their homes,
7.6 million people have been forced to flee

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:38.570
within Syria, either moving to refugee camps
or to areas that are for the moment safer,

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:41.360
and another 4 million Syrians have left the
country entirely.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.119
Of those people, about 1.6 million currently
live in Turkey; there are about a million

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:49.119
each in Lebanon and Jordan, and there are
a few hundred thousand more in Iraq and Egypt.

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:53.619
Ninety-five percent of Syrian refugees live
in those countries and they have been stretched

00:01:53.619 --> 00:01:56.740
incredibly thin by this refugee crisis.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.789
Jordan's population is now 25% refugees.

00:01:59.789 --> 00:02:04.139
You've probably seen the huge sprawling camps
in Jordan and Lebanon for refugees.

00:02:04.139 --> 00:02:07.619
And everything is completely underfunded,
because the U.N.'s Refugee Agency doesn't

00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:11.340
have nearly enough money to deal with this
number of refugees.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.569
And in Turkey, most refugees live in kind
of a legal limbo outside of camps, because

00:02:15.569 --> 00:02:18.940
Turkey doesn't expel them, but they also aren't
allowed to work.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:23.069
So even though many Syrians have good educations
and labor skills, they can't make a living,

00:02:23.069 --> 00:02:27.020
and so in search of lasting refuge, thousands
have turned to Europe.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:30.819
And they pay smugglers thousands of Euros
to get them via boat from Turkey, Morocco,

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:34.700
or Egypt, to Malta , southern Italy, or Greece's
southern islands.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:39.140
Those smugglers are essentially the only people
benefiting from Europe's inconsistent, inhumane,

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.760
and disorganized response to the refugee crisis.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:45.430
To quote the U.N.'s High Commissioner on Refugees,
"More effective international cooperation

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:49.910
is required to crack down on smugglers, including
those operating inside the EU, but in ways

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.129
that allow for the victims to be protected.

00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:55.930
But none of these efforts will be effective
without opening up more opportunities for

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:59.719
people to come legally to Europe and find
safety upon arrival."

00:02:59.719 --> 00:03:04.450
And that leads us to a very important distinction
between the words migrant and refugee.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:08.730
This has often been called a migrant crisis,
but it really isn't, because migrants choose

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:12.930
to leave their homes in search of better education,
or employment opportunities.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:18.299
Refugees, to again quote the UN HCR "are persons
fleeing armed conflict or persecution.

00:03:18.299 --> 00:03:23.050
These are people for whom the denial of asylum
has potentially deadly consequences."

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:27.890
And ever since the 1951 Refugee Commission,
refugees have had certain rights under international

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:28.890
law.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.620
These include the right not to be returned
to their country of origin if their safety

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:36.090
cannot be assured, the right not to be penalized
for entering a country illegally if they request

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:40.349
asylum, and the rights to life, security,
religious expression, primary education, free

00:03:40.349 --> 00:03:43.439
access to courts and equal treatment by taxing
authorities.

00:03:43.439 --> 00:03:48.370
If a migrant arrives illegally in the European
Union, they can be turned around and, in most

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:49.969
countries, sent home fairly quickly.

00:03:49.969 --> 00:03:54.519
But a refugee, and most of the people arriving
in Europe right now are refugees, they have

00:03:54.519 --> 00:03:59.709
certain rights under international law that
all of Europe, and basically all of the world,

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:01.920
has agreed to for the last 65 years.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.310
In short, European countries have no obligations
to refugees, until those refugees arrive in

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:08.310
Europe.

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:11.900
But once a refugee is in your country, you
have certain legal responsibilities to them

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:15.230
and that's why the boat smuggling has continued.

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:18.970
European governments want to make it difficult
for refugees to get to Europe.

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:21.280
They benefit when the trip is dangerous.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:25.430
If it were made safe, or easy, there would
be far more refugees coming to Europe.

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:30.280
The real solution, to dramatically increase
the number of refugees legally accepted through

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:34.780
non-smuggling routes, like a quota system,
well, that stuff's politically unpopular.

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:39.240
But until legal opportunities are available,
the smuggling and deaths will continue.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:44.210
To again quote the UN HCR, "Thousands of refugee
parents are risking the lives of their children

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:48.039
on unsafe smuggling boats primarily because
they have no [other] choice."

00:04:48.039 --> 00:04:51.610
And this is true, not only for Syrians, because
only about half the people seeking asylum

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:53.740
through these sea routes are from Syria.

00:04:53.740 --> 00:04:57.470
Another 12% are from Afghanistan, which was
the world's leading producer of refugees for

00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:59.510
30 years, until Syria came along.

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:02.979
Another 8% are from the northeast African
nation of Eritrea, which has one of the worst

00:05:02.979 --> 00:05:04.430
human rights records on Earth.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:08.569
Its government has been sighted by the UN
for executions, torture, forced labor and

00:05:08.569 --> 00:05:10.610
systemic rape by government officials.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:15.120
So, about 70% of the people trying to get
to Europe are from those three countries.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.910
Of course, there are also many migrants trying
to get to Europe via these dangerous over

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:19.910
water routes.

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:23.699
But, most of the people we're hearing about
on the news are refugees, and the distinction

00:05:23.699 --> 00:05:25.210
is incredibly important.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:28.340
Ok, so, we have this massive humanitarian
crisis.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:29.699
Who's to blame?

00:05:29.699 --> 00:05:30.699
Pretty much everybody.

00:05:30.699 --> 00:05:34.949
I mean, the Assad regime definitely gets a
lot of the blame, but so do Iran and Russia

00:05:34.949 --> 00:05:40.080
and China, who are providing direct, or indirect,
support to that regime and doing very little

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:41.879
about the resulting refugee crisis.

00:05:41.879 --> 00:05:45.940
The Arab states of the Gulf, although they've
pledged financial support to Syrian refugees,

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:47.620
have accepted zero.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:48.729
ZERO!

00:05:48.729 --> 00:05:49.729
refugees from Syria.

00:05:49.729 --> 00:05:54.660
Australia's refugee record is truly abysmal,
and possibly in violation of international

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:55.660
law.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:59.190
Canada is accepting 30% fewer refugees than
they were a decade ago.

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:00.850
And the United States is to blame as well.

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:05.490
We've accepted at tiny number of Syrian refugees,
fewer for instance than Brazil.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:09.259
And instead of talking seriously about how
to address the refugee crisis, our immigration

00:06:09.259 --> 00:06:11.949
debate has become increasingly racist and
irrational.

00:06:11.949 --> 00:06:15.620
For instance, you often hear in the US and
Europe, that immigrants are disproportionately

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:16.830
likely to commit crimes.

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:18.260
But that's simply untrue.

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:22.360
A huge body of data says that refugees and
first generation immigrants to the United

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:26.100
States commit crimes at a much lower rate
than other Americans.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:27.100
Ohhkay.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:28.100
And then, there's Europe.

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:32.370
The truth is the xenophobic responses to the
refugee crisis seen from some European governments

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:33.370
are just shameful.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:37.560
Like, when Hungary's Prime Minister says that
they must keep Muslims out of Europe to "keep

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.610
Europe Christian", he's not just denying the
multi-cultural and multi-religious history

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:46.629
of Europe; he's denying the international
law that requires countries to protect and

00:06:46.629 --> 00:06:49.630
house refugees regardless of their religious
beliefs.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:53.920
Hank, when discussing refugees, I often hear
"Well, it's not OUR problem", or "We have

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:55.710
to take care of OUR people."

00:06:55.710 --> 00:07:01.870
But we are one species sharing one, profoundly,
interconnected world, and humans, all humans,

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:02.990
are OUR people.

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:07.300
And when the oppressed and marginalized die
because they are oppressed and marginalized,

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:08.879
the powerful are at fault.

00:07:08.879 --> 00:07:12.470
Three year old Aylan Kurdi would be alive
today if his family would have been welcomed

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:18.080
by the European Union, or by Canada, or Australia,
or the United States, or Brazil, or the list

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.000
goes on and on.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.630
And I think the reason the world reacted so
viscerally to that image of that dead boy

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:28.710
on the beach is that instinctively, we all
knew that his blood was on all of our hands.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:33.060
We have legal obligations to refugees under
international law, but we also have ethical

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:36.050
obligations to them, because they are our
people.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:39.360
In fact, one of the reasons we're in this
mess to begin with, is that for too long we've

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.500
labored under the delusion that regional crises
have no global importance.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:48.910
Imagining any widespread human problem as
belonging to someone else is catastrophically

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:49.910
misguided.

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:53.669
Like, an Ebola outbreak in Liberia is just
a Liberian problem, right?

00:07:53.669 --> 00:07:58.280
Well, sure, until it's a Nigerian problem,
and a German problem, and an American problem.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:02.970
And as we've learned all too painfully, the
Syrian civil war is not only a Syrian problem.

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:06.410
I mean, for one thing, it's led to the growth
of ISIS, a terrorist organization that kills

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:12.620
not only Syrians and Iraqis, but Ethiopians
and Americans and Brazilians and Koreans and

00:08:12.620 --> 00:08:13.620
Turks.

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:16.659
And recently, these people in Europe, at train
stations and at football games, have been

00:08:16.659 --> 00:08:18.919
promising that refugees are welcome.

00:08:18.919 --> 00:08:22.980
It's a moving sight and an encouraging one
and I hope that European governments respond

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:23.980
in kind.

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:27.009
But the hardest work is not in making the
promise, it's keeping it.

00:08:27.009 --> 00:08:31.990
This is going to be a long term, expensive,
and complex challenge for the world.

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:37.040
And as the UN HCR has said, this massive flow
of people will not stop until the root causes

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:38.860
of their plight are addressed.

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:43.280
The ultimate solution, the only ultimate solution,
was outlined clearly for us by a thirteen

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:44.600
year old Syrian refugee.

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:46.800
"[?] Please help the Syrians.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:49.550
The Syrians need help now.

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:55.840
You, you just stop the war, and we don't go,
we don't uh, want to go to Europe.

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:58.080
Just stop the war, the Syrian."

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:02.530
Until then Hank, until this war stops, we
have a legal and moral obligation to provide

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:05.320
safe harbor for this young man and others
like him.

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:09.080
And we also must provide financial support
to the less developed countries, from Jordan

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:13.770
to Pakistan and to Iraq that are shouldering
90% of the world's refugee burden.

00:09:13.770 --> 00:09:18.140
Hank, I have included links below to donate
to the UN's refugee work, as well as Save

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:19.490
the Children's refugee work.

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:20.730
I will see you on Friday.

